{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>The daughter of the ostrich.</b> In Old French ostruce. It is called יענה on account of its being wretched, impoverished, and mournful, as it is written: ״מספד כתנים ואבל כבנות יענה״. And there is no need to prohibit it — is the ostrich itself not automatically not eaten, since it is as hard as wood, and people stay away from it? And because of its toughness it eats iron. And our Rabbis of blessed memory derived from here that the egg of an unclean bird is forbidden." "<b>The tahmas.</b> ייא״י in Old French, from the root חמס, on account of the robbery and violence in it." "<b>The shahaf.</b> אשפמר״א. And some say מוייסו״ן — it causes shahafet (wasting disease)." "<b>The nes.</b> אישטויי״ר — it has much plumage."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The Old French gloss reads אישטרוצ׳ה = ostruce (ostrich). The sentence 'ולא צריך לאסור האם היא היענה' is rendered hyper-literally; the syntax is slightly strained and may reflect textual corruption."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:11:18.475089233Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}